From 0bc69d25a5cd700c4f84fa674f7feab6952cc132 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:28:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08081/11362] [outer-loop] Wire multi-GPU layer split into the
 live generator (opt-in, degrade-safe)

CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_CUDA_GPU now accepts a comma list ("0,1") requesting a
layer split. The scalar form ("1") is byte-for-byte unchanged.

MEASURED THROUGH THE LIVE _ensure_server() PATH at the shipped n_ctx 81920, not
extrapolated from the sweep harness:

  single card  (=1)    20.47 tok/s decode,  288 prefill,  ffn_cpu=7,  1 card
  layer split  (=0,1)  38.83 tok/s decode,  908 prefill,  ffn_cpu=0,  2 cards
                       -> +89.7% decode, +215% prefill

Per-card residency 12230/12394 MiB reproduces the sweep's split2_ctx81920 arm
(12220/12384) to within ~10 MiB.

A CORRECTION I AM RECORDING RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPING. I first justified this
by claiming the single-card guard is unsatisfiable (needs 25388 MiB, a 3090 has
24576 total) and therefore "falls through to the ~2 tok/s iGPU build and the
agent runs LLM-off". The counterfactual run disproved it: the single-card path
does not fail over at all. The auto-fit spills FFN layers to system RAM until
the model fits -- landing on ffn_cpu_layers=7 -- and SUCCEEDS on the CUDA build.
Nothing errors, which is exactly why the cost was invisible: the only symptom is
half the throughput. The split avoids a forced offload; it does not rescue a
launch that would otherwise not happen. Both run; one runs degraded.

Also note the lever is NOT `-sm layer` -- that is llama.cpp's DEFAULT. It is GPU
VISIBILITY: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=str(idx) pinned the server to one card, so it
could never split whatever flags it was given. The flags are passed anyway so
`last_launch_argv` documents its own intent.

A REGRESSION CAUGHT BY READING THE CONTROL FLOW BEFORE SHIPPING. The first
version set idx = -1 whenever >1 card was requested, so a REFUSED split skipped
the single-card branch and fell straight through to the iGPU. That would have
made "0,1" strictly MORE fragile than "1": transient contention on either card
would cost the entire generator (LLM-off) instead of degrading to the ~20 tok/s
path. Ordering is now split -> single card -> iGPU, with four regression tests.

Design notes:
- `-ts` is derived from the launch env itself, not from a second read of the
  env var, so the ratio cannot be sized for a split the guard already refused.
- Per-card requirement is need_total * 1.10 / n_cards. The 1.10 is measured
  (aggregate VRAM RISES x1.048 / x1.074 when split), never a saving.
- Every requested card must clear or the whole split is refused. A narrower
  split needs MORE per card, which is the arithmetic just refused.
- Scored/Kaggle path untouched: CARNOT_LLAMA_SERVER short-circuits first with
  env=None, all devices visible, where llama.cpp already layer-splits by
  default. Deliberately not second-guessed -- never measured on L4s.

NOT ENABLED ANYWHERE. No config sets the list form, so behaviour is unchanged
until something opts in. Enabling it for the conductor would contravene the
standing 2026-06-27 allocation (conductor -> GPU 0, outer loop -> GPU 1) and is
the operator's call, not mine.

Freshness cascade handled: 6011/6012/6013/6021 REBUILT (0 substantive changes in
the first three; exp6021's three moves are provenance metadata -- mtime,
diff-stat, git_head -- which are supposed to move). The three live-GPU artifacts
with no rebuild_command got an APPENDED acknowledgement -- appended, because the
earlier pass in this session replaced that list and destroyed 7 committed links;
verified 0 lost this time. Each rebuild again dropped hand-added blocks, which
were restored and recorded.

26 new tests. Pre-existing unrelated failures in test_arc_submitted_agent_parity
and test_experiment_4744 (stale "frozen QWEN generator" assertions from the
07-28 gemma switch) verified identical at HEAD.

